Ladybird stretches text inputs, textareas, and range controls with an
automatic size and definite opposing insets, when their self-alignment
calls for stretching. Their intrinsic sizes remain the fallback for
axes that fit content, and image inputs retain replaced-element sizing.

Blink represents text fields with LayoutTextControlSingleLine and
LayoutTextControlMultiLine, both derived from LayoutBlockFlow. They use
the non-replaced path in ComputeOofInlineDimensions(), which resolves a
stretch size against the inset-modified containing block.

Gecko uses nsIFrame::ComputeAbsolutePosAutoSize(), whose shouldStretch
check accepts normal alignment for frames without HasReplacedSizing().
Its nsTextControlFrame::IntrinsicISize() supplies the intrinsic size
when stretching doesn’t apply, while Reflow() supplies the fixed block
size only if the computed block size remains unconstrained.

WebKit’s RenderTextControl derives from RenderBlockFlow. Its abspos
width follows RenderBox::computeOutOfFlowPositionedLogicalWidthUsing(),
which uses the available content space when the alignment stretches.
These paths agree on stretching resizable controls between the insets,
rather than letting intrinsic dimensions force their natural size.
